{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775347437-YDeRZbUK9pdtvtADqXpr","role":"assistant","created":1775347443,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The document doesn't appear to have a historical table of personal saving rates from 1950-1990. The 6.7% is mentioned as a \"long-term average\" but no specific year is given for when the peak occurred.\n\nLet me search more specifically within the JSON for data about historical saving rates. Maybe there's a table or text that covers the 1950-1990 period. Let me also check the Profile of the Economy section which appears to be where the personal saving data should be.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d510b3d1f5084f9eb897b17e","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1991_09.json') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\n# Look for elements around 'Personal saving' section (element 215)\nfor i in range(200, 230):\n    elem = data['document']['elements'][i]\n    content = str(elem.get('content', ''))\n    print(f'Element {i}:')\n    print(content[:2000])\n    print('===')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_81e0a0b0-e68e-46d5-8085-a69c29265a76","role":"user","created":1775347443,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_d510b3d1f5084f9eb897b17e","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element 200:\nA discussion of recent trends in corporate tax receipts, the importance of the corporate tax in foreign countries, and the effect of the Tax Reform Act of 1986 on corporate tax receipts.\n===\nElement 201:\nNone\n===\nElement 202:\nFINANCIAL OPERATIONS\n===\nElement 203:\n15\n===\nElement 204:\nProfile of the Economy\n===\nElement 205:\nGROWTH OF REAL GROSS NATIONAL PRODUCT\n===\nElement 206:\nPercent Change\n===\nElement 207:\n4th Qtr. to 4th Qtr., Quarterly Annual Rate\n===\nElement 208:\nNone\n===\nElement 209:\nReal GNP was virtually flat in the second quarter, edging down by a narrow 0.1-percent annual rate, after a 2.2-percent rate of decline during the preceding two quarters. Consumer spending and residential investment both rose, suggesting the economy was beginning to recover from the 1990-91 recession. The positive picture was offset by a weakening of the net export balance that reflected a jump in oil and other imports and a weaker balance on investment income.\n===\nElement 210:\nFEDERAL DEFICIT\n===\nElement 211:\nSum Over the Latest 12 Months\n===\nElement 212:\nNone\n===\nElement 213:\nThe Federal budget deficit in June 1991 was $2.5 billion, compared with $11.1 billion a year earlier and a record deficit of $53.3 billion in May. Much of the year-to-year decline reflected lighter spending by the Resolution Trust Corporation. Over the 12 months through\n===\nElement 214:\n16\n===\nElement 215:\nProfile of the Economy\n===\nElement 216:\nJune, the deficit totaled $235 billion, or $192 billion excluding outlays as part of the savings and loan situation. For the first 9 months of fiscal 1991, the deficit was $177 billion, compared with about $163 billion a year earlier.\n===\nElement 217:\nFEDERAL OUTLAYS AND RECEIPTS AS A SHARE OF GROSS NATIONAL PRODUCT\n===\nElement 218:\nNone\n===\nElement 219:\nFISCAL YEARS\n===\nElement 220:\nThe Federal budget outlay share of GNP averaged approximately 19 percent during the earlier postwar years, then rose to 23 percent in the 1980s. It is projected to reach a postwar high of 25 percent in fiscal 1992, including spending to deal with the savings and loan situation. The share declines to 20.2 percent by 1996, based on budget projections. Receipts were equal to 19.1 percent of GNP in fiscal 1990, and are projected to stay at 19.1 percent in the current fiscal year and to rise to 19.4 percent by 1996.\n===\nElement 221:\nPERSONAL SAVING\n===\nElement 222:\nHousehold Saving as a Percent of After-Tax Income, Through First Half 1991\n===\nElement 223:\nNone\n===\nElement 224:\nThe personal saving rate rose from a post-Depression low of 2.9 percent in 1987 to 4.6 percent in both 1989 and 1990, but remained well below the 6.7-percent long-term average. Saving appeared to be rising in early 1990, averaging 4.9 percent in the first half of the year. However, in the second half it dropped to only 4.2 percent as the slowing economy and increasing inflation reduced real incomes. The rate dipped to 3.7 percent in the second quarter of 1991, allowing only a 4-percent average for the first half of the year.\n===\nElement 225:\n17\n===\nElement 226:\nFEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\n===\nElement 227:\nINTRODUCTION\n===\nElement 228:\nBackground\n===\nElement 229:\nSection 114 of the Budget and Accounting Procedures Act of 1950 (31 U.S.C. 3513a) requires the Secretary of the Treasury to prepare reports on the financial operations of the U.S. Government.\n===","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"Element 200:\nA discussion of recent trends in corporate tax receipts, the importance of the corporate tax in foreign countries, and the effect of the Tax Reform Act of 1986 on corporate tax receipts.\n===\nElement 201:\nNone\n===\nElement 202:\nFINANCIAL OPERATIONS\n===\nElement 203:\n15\n===\nElement 204:\nProfile of the Economy\n===\nElement 205:\nGROWTH OF REAL GROSS NATIONAL PRODUCT\n===\nElement 206:\nPercent Change\n===\nElement 207:\n4th Qtr. to 4th Qtr., Quarterly Annual Rate\n===\nElement 208:\nNone\n===\nElement 209:\nReal GNP was virtually flat in the second quarter, edging down by a narrow 0.1-percent annual rate, after a 2.2-percent rate of decline during the preceding two quarters. Consumer spending and residential investment both rose, suggesting the economy was beginning to recover from the 1990-91 recession. The positive picture was offset by a weakening of the net export balance that reflected a jump in oil and other imports and a weaker balance on investment income.\n===\nElement 210:\nFEDERAL DEFICIT\n===\nElement 211:\nSum Over the Latest 12 Months\n===\nElement 212:\nNone\n===\nElement 213:\nThe Federal budget deficit in June 1991 was $2.5 billion, compared with $11.1 billion a year earlier and a record deficit of $53.3 billion in May. Much of the year-to-year decline reflected lighter spending by the Resolution Trust Corporation. Over the 12 months through\n===\nElement 214:\n16\n===\nElement 215:\nProfile of the Economy\n===\nElement 216:\nJune, the deficit totaled $235 billion, or $192 billion excluding outlays as part of the savings and loan situation. For the first 9 months of fiscal 1991, the deficit was $177 billion, compared with about $163 billion a year earlier.\n===\nElement 217:\nFEDERAL OUTLAYS AND RECEIPTS AS A SHARE OF GROSS NATIONAL PRODUCT\n===\nElement 218:\nNone\n===\nElement 219:\nFISCAL YEARS\n===\nElement 220:\nThe Federal budget outlay share of GNP averaged approximately 19 percent during the earlier postwar years, then rose to 23 percent in the 1980s. It is projected to reach a postwar high of 25 percent in fiscal 1992, including spending to deal with the savings and loan situation. The share declines to 20.2 percent by 1996, based on budget projections. Receipts were equal to 19.1 percent of GNP in fiscal 1990, and are projected to stay at 19.1 percent in the current fiscal year and to rise to 19.4 percent by 1996.\n===\nElement 221:\nPERSONAL SAVING\n===\nElement 222:\nHousehold Saving as a Percent of After-Tax Income, Through First Half 1991\n===\nElement 223:\nNone\n===\nElement 224:\nThe personal saving rate rose from a post-Depression low of 2.9 percent in 1987 to 4.6 percent in both 1989 and 1990, but remained well below the 6.7-percent long-term average. Saving appeared to be rising in early 1990, averaging 4.9 percent in the first half of the year. However, in the second half it dropped to only 4.2 percent as the slowing economy and increasing inflation reduced real incomes. The rate dipped to 3.7 percent in the second quarter of 1991, allowing only a 4-percent average for the first half of the year.\n===\nElement 225:\n17\n===\nElement 226:\nFEDERAL FISCAL OPERATIONS\n===\nElement 227:\nINTRODUCTION\n===\nElement 228:\nBackground\n===\nElement 229:\nSection 114 of the Budget and Accounting Procedures Act of 1950 (31 U.S.C. 3513a) requires the Secretary of the Treasury to prepare reports on the financial operations of the U.S. Government.\n===","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
